# ================================================================
# LINE ENDINGS — and this file exists because of a real CI failure.
#
# 2026-08-13, minutes after v0.1.0-beta.6 was tagged. `npm test` was
# green on this machine and RED on the Windows CI runner, reporting
# seven residents at once:
#
#   roles.js: 'quill' claims a chat door but is not a resident in
#             CHAT_AGENTS                    (and steward, investigator,
#             monk, computer, tutor, sebastian)
#
# CHAT_AGENTS was complete and correct. The guard in test/smoke.mjs
# finds residents by scraping the source:
#
#   /^  ([a-z]+):\{\n    label:/gm
#
# On a CRLF working tree that is `:{\r\n    label:` and the `\n` does
# not match, so it found ZERO residents and every chat role failed.
# Measured both ways rather than reasoned: LF finds all seven, CRLF
# finds none.
#
# Git stores LF and converts on checkout. This machine's working tree
# happened to be LF (files written by tooling), the Windows runner's
# was CRLF because Git for Windows defaults core.autocrlf=true — so
# the same commit was green here and red there, which is the worst
# kind of failure to chase.
#
# `eol=lf` makes every checkout on every platform match what is
# actually stored, so "works on my machine" stops being possible for
# this class of bug. test/smoke.mjs ALSO normalises at read, on
# purpose: a guard that depends on a checkout setting is not a guard,
# and the two fixes are for different failures — this one makes the
# tree consistent, that one makes the check independent of the tree.
# ================================================================

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dmg  binary
*.zip  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary

# Windows refuses to run a .bat with LF endings in some shells, and it
# is launched by double-click rather than by a test — so it is the one
# deliberate exception.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# ⚠ THE TEST FIXTURES ARE BYTE-FOR-BYTE, AND THIS LINE IS LOAD-BEARING.
#
# `git ls-files` found exactly two text files stored with CRLF in the
# index, and they are not an accident:
#
#   test/fixtures/chapters/keyword-lectures.txt      946 CRLF lines
#   test/fixtures/chapters/toc-national-parks.txt  1,576 CRLF lines
#
# They are real downloaded books, kept as they actually arrive, because
# the chapter finder was rebuilt on 2026-08-03 after the six tidy seed
# texts said it worked and 296 real ones said otherwise (CLAUDE.md
# rule 1). Real books come with CRLF. A blanket `eol=lf` would have
# quietly rewritten both fixtures into something tidier than anything a
# visitor will ever drag in — the exact tidiness that made the seed lie.
#
# `-text` means: never convert, in either direction, on any platform.
test/fixtures/** -text
